<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One Step In, Two Steps Out! – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One Step In, Two Steps Out!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
      Reading: reading_1: Show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with the teacher's guidance.<br>
      reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures.<br>
      phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
      Reading: Can show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with teacher guidance (e.g., pointing to pictures of a character walking, then jumping, and finally dancing in a story).<br>
      Can match spoken action words to their written form when supported by pictures (e.g., matching the spoken word 'dance' to a picture of a person dancing and the word 'dance').<br>
      Can recognize letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'J' in 'jump').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Dance Move Follow-Along." Students stand up and follow simple dance commands. The teacher calls out actions like "One step in!", "Two steps out!", "Spin around!", "Jump up!". The teacher uses flashcards with pictures of the actions and the written words as visual support. This activates prior vocabulary and introduces sequential commands physically.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models each dance move while saying the command clearly. For example, the teacher says, "First, take one step to the side!" (pronouncing /t/ in 'take' and /d/ in 'side') and demonstrates the action. Then, "Next, take two steps to the side!" (emphasizing 'two' and /t/ sound). The teacher shows a flashcard with the picture of the action and the written word 'step' or 'dance'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses concrete examples and gestures.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(While pointing to a picture of a person dancing) "Is she dancing or sleeping?" (Expected: "Dancing!")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After demonstrating "one step in") "Did I take one step *in* or one step *out*?" (Expected: "In!")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the word 'jump') "What is this word? Show me 'jump'!" (Students demonstrate the action).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated sequence of large picture cards or a big storybook titled "Our Dance Adventure" (narrative excerpt). Each page/card shows a character (e.g., a friendly bear) performing a dance move, accompanied by a simple sentence or command. Examples: "First, the bear hops. One hop!", "Next, the bear spins around. Spin fast!", "Then, the bear takes two steps to the side. One, two steps!", "Now, the bear jumps over the line!". The text explicitly uses sequential commands, numbers, action verbs, prepositions, and target vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension: Understanding simple narrative sequencing and matching written words to pictures/actions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to words as they are read. After each "page," the teacher asks, "What does the bear do now?" (Expected: "Hops!" or "Spins!").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pointing to sequencing:</b> The teacher reads the entire story once. Then, the teacher holds up all the picture cards out of order. "Who can point to what the bear does *first*?" "Who can point to what the bear does *next*?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (word to picture):</b> The teacher isolates specific action word flashcards (e.g., 'jump', 'spin', 'step', 'dance'). The teacher reads a word, and students point to the corresponding picture in the storybook/on the cards. The teacher highlights letters like 'J' in 'jump', 'S' in 'spin', 'T' in 'two' while pointing.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of action words and sequential commands, recognizing letters of the alphabet (J, S, T, U, V), and matching words to pictures.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Sequential commands with numbers ('First, one step'), simple directive phrases ('Move to the front'), positional commands with prepositions ('Stand next to the chair', 'Jump over the line').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> move, dance, step, spin, turn, twirl, shake, hop, put, jump, one, two, in, out, around, next to, over.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /t/ in 'two', /d/ in 'dance'.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing letters J, S, T, U, V.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ol>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Word & Picture Match":</b> The teacher distributes small sets of flashcards to each student or small group. Each set contains simple pictures of dance moves (e.g., 'jump', 'spin', 'step in', 'step out') and separate word cards with their corresponding written form. Students are instructed to match each picture card with its correct word card. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting: "Can you find 'jump'? Where is the word 'jump'?" Teacher draws attention to the letter 'J' in 'jump'.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Listen & Point":</b> The teacher projects or holds up a large poster with 3-4 sequential action pictures (e.g., 'First, one step in the circle.', 'Next, jump over the line.'). Below each picture, the corresponding sentence is written. The teacher reads a sentence aloud (e.g., "Jump over the line!"), and students point to the correct picture AND point to the word 'jump' or 'line' in the sentence. The teacher guides pronunciation of /d/ in 'dance' and /t/ in 'two' when reading these commands. The teacher introduces and points to letters S, T, U, V on flashcards and in target words if present.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Read & Show":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 simple picture cards with a single action word printed underneath (e.g., "Dance!", "Spin!", "Hop!"). One student reads the word aloud (or attempts to), and the other student points to the picture and acts out the movement. They switch roles. The teacher encourages polite expressions like "go ahead" when taking turns.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will engage in a "Story Sequencing Dance" task. They work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a larger set of 4-5 illustrated story cards that depict a short, simple dance sequence (e.g., "The Little Cat Learns to Dance"). Each card has a simple action word/phrase underneath (e.g., "First, step!", "Next, spin!", "Then, jump!", "Finally, dance!"). The task is for the group to arrange the cards in the correct sequence to tell the story. As they arrange them, they must point to the action word on each card and try to identify familiar letters like 'J' in 'jump' or 'S' in 'spin'. After sequencing, they will briefly "read" their sequence to the teacher by pointing to the pictures and the words, with teacher guidance. This activity focuses on reading comprehension of story sequencing and matching written words to actions and pictures within the "Let's Dance!" theme.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Story Sequencing Dance" activity. The teacher will observe each student's ability to sequence the picture cards, point to action words, and recognize familiar letters. The teacher can also use a simple anecdotal record.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually receive a small worksheet with 3-4 jumbled pictures depicting a simple dance sequence from the story. Below each picture, there is a simple action word from the story (e.g., 'step', 'jump', 'dance'). Students cut and paste the pictures in the correct sequence in boxes labeled '1st', '2nd', '3rd'. They then circle the letters 'J' in 'jump' and 'D' in 'dance' on their worksheet.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct sequencing of pictures (Shows comprehension of story sequencing).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to point to/identify action words next to pictures.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of familiar letters (e.g., 'J' in 'jump', 'D' in 'dance').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group work (Sociolinguistic).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up or thumbs down.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I put the dance pictures in order?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I find the word 'jump'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move word to find today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word was a little bit hard to find?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students understand story sequencing and match words to pictures? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, small group work).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with identifying words or sequencing? (e.g., more direct letter recognition practice, simpler stories, one-on-one guidance).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better integrate the /t/ and /d/ pronunciation points in future reading activities for Kinder?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on the reading comprehension of dance commands and action words by having students actively speak and give these commands to each other in pairs, using the vocabulary and sequential structures learned today.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center;">Date: <br> Time: 2-3 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>